 Member 3 (Pediatric Gastroenterologist): Addresses gastrointestinal symptoms, particularly chronic diarrhea in infants. This clinician will evaluate for potential gastrointestinal causes of immune dysfunction and related nutritional concerns due to weight status.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Clinical Laboratory Scientist (Lead)): Responsible for overseeing laboratory tests, including the interpretation of immunological assays that can identify specific antibody deficiencies or T
 Member 2 (Pediatric Hematologist): Engages in the assessment of hematological parameters and correlates these with immunological function. This clinician will provide insight on any blood disorders that may contribute to the child's immunodeficiency.
 Member 3 (Pediatric Radiologist): An expert in imaging pediatric patients, will assess relevant imaging studies to ensure there are no structural anomalies contributing to the patient's recurrent infections or symptoms.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Pediatric Immunologist (Lead)): Provides final expertise on the interpretation of findings from both IAT and DET, and determines the most probable immunodeficiency diagnosis.
 Member 2 (Senior Pediatrician): Offers a comprehensive review of the child’s medical history, symptoms, and examination findings to assist in a holistic diagnosis and treatment approach.
